```java
class Cart {
    List<String> items;
    Cart(List<String> items) { this.items = items; }
    Cart shallowCopy() { return new Cart(this.items); } // same list reference!
}

Cart original = new Cart(new ArrayList<>(List.of("apple")));
Cart copy = original.shallowCopy();
copy.items.add("banana");

original.items; // ["apple", "banana"] — the "copy"'s mutation leaked back in
```

`copy` and `original` are different `Cart` objects, but `copy.items` and `original.items` are the *same list object* in memory. Adding to one is adding to both — a bug that looks like a haunted, action-at-a-distance mutation if you don't already know to suspect shallow copying.

```java
class Cart {
    List<String> items;
    Cart(List<String> items) { this.items = items; }
    Cart deepCopy() { return new Cart(new ArrayList<>(this.items)); } // new list
}

Cart original = new Cart(new ArrayList<>(List.of("apple")));
Cart copy = original.deepCopy();
copy.items.add("banana");

original.items; // ["apple"] — unaffected, copy has its own independent list
```

`new ArrayList<>(this.items)` constructs a genuinely separate list with copied elements, so `copy.items` and `original.items` no longer point at the same object. Mutating one has no effect on the other — the independence the caller actually expected from "copy."
